1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:02,180
好了，咱们直接进入正题。
Alright, let's just dive straight into this.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:07,812
今天我们要讲解一个全新的内容，它涵盖了人工智能领域的一次彻底的范式转变。
Today, we're going through a brand new explainer covering a literal paradigm shift in artificial intelligence.

3
00:00:08,141 --> 00:00:14,404
说真的，这项研究挑战了我们关于 AI 如何学习世界的几乎所有认知。
Seriously, this research challenges basically everything we thought we knew about how AI actually learns about our world.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:23,716
你知道，多年来，我们的策略一直是暴力破解，就是把堆
积如山的各种数据塞给 AI，然后祈祷它能搞清楚状况。
You know, for years now, the strategy has just been brute force, throwing mountains and
mountains of data at an AI and just crossing our fingers that it figures things out.

5
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:29,412
好，大家坐稳了，因为这项新研究完全颠覆了这种暴力破解的方法。
Well, hold on to your hats because this new research it completely flips that brute force approach on its head.

6
00:00:30,126 --> 00:00:32,800
所以我们要从一个相当深刻的谜题开始。
So we're going to start with a pretty profound mystery.

7
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:42,084
稍微思考一下：一个看图片的视觉模型和一个读段落的文本模型，最终理解的是完全相同的现实。
Just think about this for a second: a vision model looking at a picture and a text
model reading a paragraph eventually come to understand the exact same reality.

8
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:48,640
但目前，他们只有在消耗了极其庞大、令人难以置信的数据量后，才能做到这一点。
But right now, they only manage to do that after consuming absolutely massive, mind-boggling amounts of data.

9
00:00:48,910 --> 00:00:50,692
我们说的是数万亿个 token。
We're talking trillions of tokens here.

10
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:54,116
这到底为什么效率低到如此地步？
Why in the world is it so incredibly inefficient?

11
00:00:54,830 --> 00:01:00,400
嗯，为了回答这个问题，我们必须看看一个叫 Platonic
Representation Hypothesis，也就是 PRH 的东西。
Well, to answer that, we have to look at something called the Platonic Representation Hypothesis, or PRH.

12
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:10,080
基本上，这个假设表明，在所有这些东西的底层，不同的 AI 模型，
无论它们是基于像素还是文本训练的，最终看到的都是同一个世界。
Basically, this hypothesis suggests that underneath it all, different AI models,
whether they're trained on pixels or text, are eventually seeing the exact same world.

13
00:01:10,414 --> 00:01:14,692
这是一个植根于古代哲学、特别是柏拉图的“洞穴寓言”中非常酷的想法。
It's this super cool idea rooted in ancient philosophy, specifically Plato's Cave.

14
00:01:14,958 --> 00:01:15,972
但关键点来了。
But here's the kicker.

15
00:01:16,302 --> 00:01:22,788
直到最近，它还只是一个假设，你知道，只是一个没有任何硬核数学证明的观察结果。
Until very recently, it was just a hypothesis, you know, just an observation without any real hard mathematical proof.

16
00:01:23,662 --> 00:01:28,420
从古代哲学到现代神经网络，柏拉图式的 AI 之梦。
The Platonic AI dream, from ancient philosophy to modern neural networks.

17
00:01:29,198 --> 00:01:30,628
好，还记得柏拉图的洞穴吗？
Okay, so remember Plato's Cave?

18
00:01:31,054 --> 00:01:34,660
囚犯们只能看到投射在墙上的真实物体的影子。
The prisoners only see shadows of real objects cast on a wall.

19
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:38,320
现在，这些影子看起来完全不同，取决于光线和角度，对吧？
Now, those shadows look totally different depending on the light and the angle, right?

20
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:41,540
但投射出这些影子的实际物理对象是完全一样的。
But the actual physical object casting them is the exact same.

21
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:53,412
研究人员认为，无论 AI 是在读描述性的句子还是在看高分辨率照片，
它只是在观察同一个底层现实的不同影子或渲染结果。
Well, the researchers are arguing that whether an AI reads a descriptive sentence or looks at a high-res photo,
it's just observing different shadows or renderings of the exact same underlying reality.

22
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,780
他们把这种现实称为“世界状态”。
They call this reality the world state.

23
00:01:56,206 --> 00:01:57,668
而 AI 的终极目标是什么？
And the AI's ultimate goal?

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,700
是去理解那个真实的“世界状态”，而不仅仅是记住一堆影子。
To understand that true world state, not just memorize a bunch of shadows.

25
00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:06,020
下一个 token 的预测问题，以及现状的低效。
The next token problem, the inefficiency of the status quo.

26
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:09,860
这篇论文背后的研究人员对此的表述非常直白。
The researchers behind this paper are surprisingly blunt about this.

27
00:02:10,286 --> 00:02:15,812
他们直接指出，预测下一个 token 是通往 Platonic Representation 的一条极其低效的路径。
They straight up state that next token prediction is a remarkably inefficient route to platonic representations.

28
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:18,020
那么，为什么这件大事意义重大呢？
Now, why is that such a big deal?

29
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:24,644
因为预测下一个 token 简直就是驱动我们今天使用的几乎每一个巨型语言模型的暴力破解引擎。
Because next token prediction is literally the brute force engine driving almost every single massive language model we use today.

30
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:31,760
我们基本上是在一个从根本上无法高效理解现实的基础之上，构建我们最先进的 AI。
We are essentially building our absolute most advanced AI on a foundation that is fundamentally inefficient at understanding reality.

31
00:02:32,046 --> 00:02:32,612
挺疯狂的，对吧？
Crazy, right?

32
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:37,316
要真正理解为什么它如此低效，我们得聊聊「干扰项」。
To really grasp why it's so inefficient, we have to talk about nuisance.

33
00:02:37,934 --> 00:02:41,028
干扰项仅仅是数据表面的格式化内容。
Nuisance is just the superficial formatting of a piece of data.

34
00:02:41,454 --> 00:02:46,308
它是作者特定的措辞，或者是相机特定的光影和角度。
It's an author's specific word choice, or the specific lighting and angle of a camera.

35
00:02:46,862 --> 00:03:00,452
当我们强迫 AI 去猜测单个文档中的下一个词时，它会浪费大量的脑力去记忆这些干扰项，
也就是那个特定文件的特定风格，而不是去学习真实的底层世界。
When we force an AI to just guess the next word in a single document, it wastes an unbelievable amount of brain power memorizing this
nuisance, the specific style of that one single file, instead of learning about the actual underlying world.

36
00:03:00,814 --> 00:03:02,756
它完全被影子给分散了注意力。
It gets completely distracted by the shadow.

37
00:03:03,598 --> 00:03:08,000
正因如此，NextToken 预测就像研究人员所说的「糟糕的视图」。
Because of this, NextToken prediction acts as what the researchers call a bad view.

38
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:13,920
它强迫 AI 去看一个前缀，也就是句子中的几个词，然后去预测它的直接后续。
It forces the AI to look at a prefix, just a few words of a sentence, and predict its own direct continuation.

39
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,924
AI 被锁死在这个单个文档的微小、狭隘的语境中。
The AI gets locked into this tiny, narrow context of a single document.

40
00:03:18,318 --> 00:03:23,300
它是在强迫 AI 去重叠同一事物的损坏信息，而不是去理解更广泛的场景。
It's forcing the AI to just overlap corruptions of one thing rather than understanding the broader scene.

41
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:25,764
这基本上就是把一段描述和它自己配对。
It's basically pairing a caption with itself.

42
00:03:26,222 --> 00:03:31,684
而要用这种方式去理解更广阔的世界，你就需要海量且密集的重叠数据。
And to make sense of the wider world that way, you need a ridiculously massive, dense amount of overlapping data.

43
00:03:32,526 --> 00:03:33,636
那么，我们该如何解决呢？
So, how do we fix it?

44
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:39,140
这就引出了第三部分：良好视图对比糟糕视图，这是范式转移的框架。
That brings us to section three: good views versus bad views, the framework for a paradigm shift.

45
00:03:39,854 --> 00:03:42,720
这里的核心秘诀在于我们如何配对数据。
The real secret sauce here is in how we pair the data.

46
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:47,044
良好视图使用对完全相同的场景的两种完全不同的观察。
Good views use two completely different observations of the exact same scene.

47
00:03:47,470 --> 00:03:52,420
想象一下，有两个完全不同的描述，由两个不同的人写成，描述的是同一张照片。
Picture two totally different captions, written by two different people describing the exact same photograph.

48
00:03:52,846 --> 00:04:02,500
另一方面，糟糕视图使用的是对单一观察的两种损坏，比如拿一个句子，
隐藏几个词去预测剩下的内容，这正是 NextToken 预测所做的事情。
Bad views, on the other hand, use two corruptions of a single observation, like taking one sentence and just hiding a few words to
predict the rest, which is exactly what next token prediction does.

49
00:04:02,990 --> 00:04:07,620
良好视图剥离了那些干扰项，强迫 AI 专注于真实的客观状态。
Good views strip away that nuisance and force the AI to focus on the true world state.

50
00:04:07,982 --> 00:04:12,612
糟糕视图只是把 AI 困在单一观察的表面格式中。
Bad views, they just trap the AI in the superficial formatting of a single observation.

51
00:04:13,294 --> 00:04:17,440
有了良好视图，模型会将场景的每一种描述连接起来，而不是其他的。
With good views, the model joins every description of a scene and never another.

52
00:04:17,678 --> 00:04:21,476
通过设计，它能瞬间锁定世界的真实共享内核。
By design, it instantly locks onto the true shared kernel of the world.

53
00:04:21,934 --> 00:04:29,828
糟糕视图大多只是把一段描述和它自己配对，这意味着它们
只能通过纯粹的瞎猜和海量的重叠数据来连接到不同的场景。
Bad views mostly just pair a caption with itself, meaning they only connect to
different scenes through sheer dumb chance and massive amounts of overlapping data.

54
00:04:30,094 --> 00:04:33,060
所以，良好视图通过设计修复了世界内核。
So good views fix the world kernel by design.

55
00:04:33,422 --> 00:04:37,764
糟糕视图只是祈祷它们最终能通过暴力破解偶然撞见它。
Bad views just cross their fingers and hope they eventually stumble upon it through brute force.

56
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:48,256
因此，通过使用这些良好视图来训练联合嵌入预测架构，也就是 JEPA，
你从数学上保证了它与现实对齐。
So, by training a joint embedding predictive architecture, or JEPA, using these good views,
you mathematically guarantee that it aligns with reality.

57
00:04:48,494 --> 00:04:49,668
它忽略了干扰项。
It ignores the nuisance.

58
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:52,336
它完全专注于共享的世界状态。
It focuses entirely on the shared world state.

59
00:04:52,590 --> 00:04:54,180
而且这一切都是通过设计实现的。
And it does this all by design.

60
00:04:54,638 --> 00:05:01,732
因为它不依赖这些巨量数据集中的偶然重叠，它完全消除了对天文数字般海量数据的需求。
Because it's not relying on chance overlaps in these giant data sets,
it completely eliminates the need for astronomical amounts of data.

61
00:05:02,062 --> 00:05:07,812
我的意思是，研究人员在这里简直是把 Platonic
Representation Hypothesis 变成了经过证实的数学定理。
I mean, the researchers literally turned the Platonic AI hypothesis into a proven mathematical theorem here.

62
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,136
好了，第四部分，效率的突破。
All right, section four, the efficiency breakthrough.

63
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:13,892
微型 JEPA 对抗巨型 LLM。
A tiny Jepa takes on the giant LLMs.

64
00:05:14,702 --> 00:05:17,536
42。我希望你们记住这个数字。
42. I want you to remember that number.

65
00:05:17,998 --> 00:05:30,576
通过利用这些良好视图，JEPA 模型达到了与标准下一词预测模型完全相同的对齐水平，
但它使用的训练样本却惊人地减少了 42 倍。
By utilizing these good views, the Jepa model reached the exact same level of alignment as a standard next token prediction model,
but it did so using an astonishing 42 times fewer training samples.

66
00:05:30,926 --> 00:05:32,484
让这个信息在脑子里过一下。
Just let that sink in for a second.

67
00:05:32,878 --> 00:05:37,220
仅仅通过改变数据的配对方式，样本效率就提高了 42 倍。
42 times more sample efficient purely by changing how the data was paired.

68
00:05:37,902 --> 00:05:41,856
这建立了一个真正令人难以置信的“大卫对阵歌利亚”的对比。
This sets up a truly incredible David versus Goliath comparison.

69
00:05:42,126 --> 00:05:48,228
一边是我们的“大卫”，一个微型、高效、拥有 1.09 亿参数的良好视图 JEPA 模型。
On one side, we have our David, a tiny, highly efficient, 109 million parameter Goodview JEPA model.

70
00:05:48,718 --> 00:05:56,132
另一边是“歌利亚”，巨大的、拥有 720 亿参数的传统语言模型，比如 Quen 2.5 和 Llama 3。
On the other side, Goliath, massive 72 billion parameter traditional language models, like Quen 2.5 and Llama 3.

71
00:05:56,526 --> 00:06:01,056
这些巨头完全依赖于暴力规模和数万亿的 token。
These giants rely entirely on brute force scale and trillions of tokens.

72
00:06:01,358 --> 00:06:04,996
所以研究人员把它们放在一起进行头对头的对比，看看谁真正更懂这个世界。
So the researchers put them head-to-head to see who actually understands the world better.

73
00:06:05,646 --> 00:06:09,316
当你看到数据时，最后的证明简直太疯狂了。
And the final proof is absolutely wild when you look at the data.

74
00:06:09,646 --> 00:06:15,588
我们微型的良好视图 JEPA 的性能完全超越了巨大的 Quen 和 Llama 模型。
The performance of our tiny Goodview Jeppa completely skyrockets past the massive Quinn and Llama models.

75
00:06:16,046 --> 00:06:24,976
我们说的是一个微型模型，全面击败了比它大 660 倍、
并且在 15 到 18 万亿 token 上训练出来的巨头。
We're talking about a tiny model, comprehensively beating giants that
are 660 times larger and were trained on 15 to 18 trillion tokens.

76
00:06:25,326 --> 00:06:29,732
用正确方式训练的微型模型，简直碾压了用错误方式训练的巨头。
The tiny model trained the right way simply crushes the giants trained the wrong way.

77
00:06:30,414 --> 00:06:33,536
而且，嘿，这些不仅仅是图表上的理论数字。
And hey, these aren't just theoretical numbers on some chart.

78
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:37,056
这种方法转化成了巨大的现实世界统治力。
This approach translates into massive real-world dominance.

79
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:46,016
这个只有 1.09 亿参数的微型模型实现了一个图像描述器，
在行业标准 Coco Visual Benchmark 上获得了惊人的 0.838 分。
This tiny 109 million parameter model enables an image captioner that
scores an incredible 0.838 on the industry standard Coco Visual Benchmark.

80
00:06:46,190 --> 00:06:50,244
而且它在网络训练中甚至没有使用过任何图像描述对。
And it does that without a single image caption pair used in its network training.

81
00:06:50,576 --> 00:06:56,100
此外，在 ImageNet 100 图像识别测试中，它的零样本准确率达到了 64%。
Plus, on the ImageNet 100 image recognition test, it hits a 64% zero shot accuracy.

82
00:06:56,656 --> 00:07:01,056
这绝对碾压了只能达到 56% 的 80 亿参数模型。
That absolutely crushes 8 billion parameter models that could only hit 56%.

83
00:07:01,646 --> 00:07:04,068
它在理解世界方面客观上更好。
It is just objectively better at understanding the world.

84
00:07:04,782 --> 00:07:07,616
这就带到了今天讲解的核心结论。
Which brings us to the ultimate takeaway from today's explainer.

85
00:07:08,046 --> 00:07:11,616
Platonic Representation Hypothesis 不再仅仅是一个猜测。
The platonic representation hypothesis is no longer just a guess.

86
00:07:11,790 --> 00:07:14,180
它是一个经过证实的定理，也是一个极具实用性的工具。
It's a proven theorem and a highly practical tool.

87
00:07:14,496 --> 00:07:16,516
但这引出了一个非常重大的问题。
But it begs a really massive question.

88
00:07:16,846 --> 00:07:28,132
如果用正确方式训练的 1.09 亿参数微型模型可以轻松击败用错误方式训练的 720 亿参数巨头，
那么暴力规模 AI 扩张的时代是否终于结束了？
If a tiny 109 million parameter model trained the right way can effortlessly beat a 72 billion parameter giant trained the wrong way,
is the era of brute force AI scaling finally over?

89
00:07:28,782 --> 00:07:34,148
我把这个思考留给你们，让我们一起见证真正的 Platonic AI 未来如何展开。
I'll leave you with that thought to ponder as we watch the future of true platonic AI unfold.

90
00:07:34,414 --> 00:07:35,108
感谢加入我。
Thanks for joining me.

